<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">São Tomé and Príncipe</h1><p class="st-meta">1975 CE – present · capital São Tomé</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An island country in the Gulf of Guinea, independent from Portugal in 1975, a one-party state until 1990 and a multiparty democracy since.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-sao-tome-and-principe">Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe</a> became independent on 12 July 1975, after Portugal's Caetano <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> was overthrown in April 1974 and its new government agreed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in November 1974 to transfer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe (MLSTP) chose its Secretary General, Manuel Pinto da Costa, as the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The country was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> under the MLSTP until 1990, when it became one of the first African countries to undergo democratic reform. Opposition parties were made legal, and elections in 1991 were non-violent, free and transparent; Miguel Trovoada won them as an independent candidate. Fradique de Menezes was elected in 2001, and in July 2003 the army seized power for one week before an accord returned him to office. A coup attempt against him in 2009 failed. The islands' economy had been built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa grown with enslaved labour.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Evaristo Carvalho became president in 2016, and Carlos Vila Nova won the presidential election in September 2021. In September 2022 the Independent Democratic Action party, led by Patrice Trovoada, won the legislative election, and in November 2022 an attempted coup was thwarted. The country is a unitary state with a semi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a> and a 55-member National Assembly.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sao-tome-and-principe.jpg" alt="São Tomé and Príncipe" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_São_Tomé_and_Príncipe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1975 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>São Tomé</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Pinto da Costa, Miguel Trovoada, Fradique de Menezes, Evaristo Carvalho, Carlos Vila Nova</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>the Catholic Church, Protestant churches, small and growing Muslim population</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Forro and other Portuguese creoles</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>São Tomé and Príncipe</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Democratic Republic of São Tomé and Príncipe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=sao-tome-and-principe" aria-label="São Tomé and Príncipe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where São Tomé and Príncipe lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5574 4971L5574 4971"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5574 4971h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5574 4971h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5574 4971h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks São Tomé. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=sao-tome-and-principe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
